# Example .gitignore for a python project
#
# Files that you put here, will NOT be included when you commit your chenges

# Exclude a single file:
data/a_very_large_file.zip

# Using patterns, we can exclude groups of files that we know we'd never want to commit:
*.zip
*.pyc
*.jpeg
*.jpg
*.png
*.gif
*.pdf
# Note that:
# *.zip matches all .zip-files everywhere
# **/*.zip Explicitly matches all .zip-files in every sub-diretory
# /*.zip Matches .zip-files ONLY in the first directory (file.zip, but not files/file.zip)
# files/*.zip matches all .zip-files inside the `files` directory

# Git is TEXT-based, but not all file formats that contain text is text. Binary file-formats should be ignored also:
*.doc
*.docx
*.xls
*.xlsx
*.ods
*.pages
*.ppx

# In your git-repository. You should keep .md for text, .csv for data and .tex (latex) for documents
# Building tex-files, however, generates some nasty cache-files which we don't need to share with our colaborators.
# LaTeX build artifacts
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.lof
*.lot
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.bbl
*.blg
*.bcf
*.run.xml

# If indeed, we do want some small images, we can reverse the ignore with !
!images/a_small_icon.png

# Python projects sometime leave cache files around too
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# These files are OS-specific. Macs, for example, keeps preferences on how windows are displayed in folders in a hidden file called .DS_Store
.DS_Store
**.exe

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# IDE:s and Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.vscode/
.idea/